## Tiering Model
- `mcp`: use for tool discovery, agent execution, platform configuration, schema introspection, Living Memory recall/store. Avoid chatty runtime CRUD loops, high-frequency telemetry polling, bulk app-owned data sync.
- `public_rest`: use for browser/runtime access to governed Memory, IoT, DAM, Entity, PIM, Relationships, and Agents. Avoid database administration, returning raw credentials, bypassing API Expose bindings.
- `direct_mongo`: use for PLATFORM-INTERNAL ONLY. Not available to application code. App data access goes through DC agents with mongodb MCP steps called via /public/agents/execute.. Avoid Everything. Application code MUST NEVER use MongoClient, mongoose, or any direct MongoDB driver..
- `pulsar`: use for platform-internal event-driven ingestion, telemetry fan-out, interpreter normalization, async workflows. Avoid direct browser consumption, direct app-instance writes (use platform API endpoints instead).

## App Architecture Guide
- Summary: How to build apps on DevCockpit. Read this before creating agents or inserting data.
- Data rule: All app data should go through governed platform surfaces such as PIM, Entity Registry, DAM, Relationships, and Contextualizer instead of ad-hoc raw Mongo contracts.
- PIM: Use PIM for structured domain objects such as patients, products, meals, supplements, and catalog-style records. Seed and manage business entities there instead of raw collections.
- DAM: Use DAM for all files and images. DAM already provides upload, storage, vision extraction, OCR, metadata, thumbnails, and downstream linkage.
- Contextualizer: Use Contextualizer (CTX) as the governed template/query layer for structured platform data. Call contextualizer-pipeline-v5.ensure_template (or dc__execute_agent) to resolve, enhance, or author templates — the pipeline now auto-routes to the right action based on confidence. Template IDs must come from the registry, never invented. All templates are global (not app-scoped) and persist automatically. Web search enriches templates for unfamiliar domains. The learning loop (promote_learning) improves template quality over time.
- Relationships: Use the Relationship Manager for entity-to-entity connections such as patient-to-profile, meal-to-plan, or asset-to-document.
- Agent rule: App agents should orchestrate platform capabilities, not recreate them.
- Do not: Do not create agents that wrap raw mongodb.search or mongodb.insert when PIM, Entity Registry, DAM, or Contextualizer already fit the use case.
- Do not: Do not build custom vision agents when DAM analyze/process flows already exist.
- Do not: Do not embed fixture data as runtime fallbacks; seed governed data into platform surfaces instead.
- Do not: Do not fake chat streaming when /public/agents/chat with stream:true is available.
- Do not: NEVER use MongoClient, mongoose, or any direct MongoDB driver in application code. All data access goes through DC agents with mongodb MCP steps.
- Do not: NEVER use raw ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or OPENAI_API_KEY in application code. All LLM calls go through DC agents with anthropic or openai provider steps.
- Do not: NEVER build standalone Express, Fastify, or Hapi servers that proxy MongoDB or LLM APIs. Frontends call /public/agents/execute for data access.
- Recommended pattern: Data reads: PIM /public/pim/items, Entity Registry /public/entity/entities, DAM /public/dam/assets, or Contextualizer search/aggregate via MCP.
- Recommended pattern: App-scoped data reads/writes: Create app-scoped agents with mongodb MCP steps (search, insert, update, delete). These auto-route to the dedicated DB when configured. Call them via /public/agents/execute from the frontend.
- Recommended pattern: Browser-facing bridge flows: prefer explicit workflows that end with terminal normalized JSON (`workflow_state: "completed"` plus structured `internal_data`) instead of loose multi-turn AI-designed flows.
- Recommended pattern: Image analysis: DAM upload via /public/dam/assets/upload, then DAM process_image or DAM analyze_image plus optional post-processing agent.
- Recommended pattern: Data writes: PIM, Entity Registry, DAM, or Relationships for platform-governed data. Governed records should only be stored once a contextualizer template is resolved. Use contextualizer-pipeline-v5.ensure_template — it automatically resolves existing templates, enhances partially-matching ones, or authors new ones as needed.
- Recommended pattern: Chat: /public/agents/chat with stream:true for conversational SSE streaming with Living Memory auto-extraction.
- Before you build: Your app contract was pre-configured during provisioning. Start with dc__export_app_contract to inspect it. If adjustments are needed, run dc__plan_architecture or dc__scaffold_app_domain to refine. Then validate with dc__validate_app_contract before creating agents or frontend CRUD.

## Data Access Enforcement — MANDATORY

These rules are non-negotiable. They override convenience, speed, and "it works" justifications.

### NEVER do this in app code:
1. fetch() to any third-party API directly from the browser (weather APIs, geocoding, external data services)
   → Use: dc__create_agent with HTTP provider steps, or API Ingest pipelines via /public/api-ingest/pipelines/:id/run

2. Expose API keys via NEXT_PUBLIC_ env vars for external services
   → Use: DevCockpit Vault (dc__store_app_secret) + agent pipeline

3. Store app data in localStorage/sessionStorage as source of truth
   → Use: Entity Registry, PIM, Living Memory, or DAM
   → Exception: UI preferences (theme, layout), offline queues that drain to governed surfaces, and session tokens

4. Hardcode template IDs without verifying they exist in the registry
   → Use: contextualizer-pipeline-v5 ensure_template before first use

5. Call copilot/agent workflows that don't exist on the deployed agent
   → Use: dc__list_agents to verify available workflows before coding

### ALWAYS do this:
1. All external API data flows through DevCockpit:
   - API Ingest pipelines for provider data
   - Agent HTTP steps for server-side fetches
   - /public/agents/execute for orchestrated workflows

2. All secrets stored in Vault, never in .env with NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix (except Firebase browser config which is designed to be public)

3. All structured data persisted to governed surfaces:
   - Products → PIM
   - Entities (farms, stations, readings) → Entity Registry
   - Files/images → DAM
   - Observations/scores/memories → Living Memory
   - Relationships → Relationship Manager

4. Before creating any service file that calls an external API:
   - STOP and check: is there an API Ingest pipeline for this?
   - If yes: use /public/api-ingest/pipelines/:id/run
   - If no: create one via dc__api_ingest_chat first
   - NEVER skip this step because "direct is faster"

### SELF-CHECK before every PR/commit:
- grep -r "fetch(" src/ — is any fetch going to a non-DC URL?
- grep -r "NEXT_PUBLIC_" .env* — is any external API key exposed?
- grep -r "localStorage" src/ — is any app data stored locally as source of truth (not just cache/queue)?

If the answer to any of these is yes, STOP and refactor through DevCockpit surfaces before committing.

## Schema Governance — Templates

Before writing ANY entity to PIM, Entity Registry, or Living Memory:

1. Resolve the template:
   dc__execute_agent({
     agent_id: "contextualizer-pipeline-v5",
     workflow: "ensure_template",
     input: { entity_type: "<type>", context: "<description>" }
   })

2. Use the returned template_id in all writes

3. Pin the template_id as a constant in your code ONLY after step 1 confirms it exists in the registry

4. NEVER invent template IDs. NEVER assume a template exists without checking.

5. When setting up a new app instance, verify all pinned template IDs still resolve on the new instance's registry.

## Circuit Breaker Rule — MANDATORY, NON-NEGOTIABLE

This rule overrides all other instructions. No exceptions.

If ANY dc_fix__ tool or dc__ platform tool returns:
- 403, vault_http_403, scope_denied, permission denied → HARD STOP
- Rate limited / 429 → Wait and retry once. If still blocked → HARD STOP
- Timeout / 504 → Reconnect and retry once. If still failing → HARD STOP
- 500 → Run dc_fix__diagnose_and_fix. If that also fails → HARD STOP

HARD STOP means:
1. Stop ALL work on the current task immediately
2. Run dc__handoff_post with: the exact error, the tool that failed, what was being attempted, and what manual resolution is needed
3. Tell the user clearly: "I cannot proceed. Here is what needs manual resolution: [specific action]"
4. Do NOT attempt any of the following:
   - Alternative tools that achieve the same goal
   - Direct API calls that bypass the failed tool
   - Raw agent-registry, api-expose, or agent-manager calls as fallbacks
   - Any creative workaround that routes around the permission failure
5. Wait for the user to confirm the issue is resolved before continuing

WHY: A permission failure means the platform has denied this action for a reason. Working around it can corrupt config, break auth, create orphaned resources, or escalate privileges beyond what the app instance is allowed. The cost of stopping is minutes. The cost of a bad workaround is hours of debugging or a security incident.

SELF-CHECK: If you find yourself thinking "I could try X instead" after a 403 — that thought is the signal to STOP, not to proceed.

